doctype html
html(lang="en")
  head
    meta(charset="UTF-8")
    meta(name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0")
    link(rel="stylesheet" href="/css/main.css")
    title Play Area
  body
    .hmrc-wrap(style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.5; ")
        .hmrc-header
            img(src="/images/hmrc-logo-plain.webp" alt="Logo")
            div(style="text-align: right; margin-bottom: 20px;")
                p(style="margin: 0;")
                    strong Anthony Yates
                p(style="margin: 0;") Your Address Line 1
                p(style="margin: 0;") Your Address Line 2
                p(style="margin: 0;") City, Postcode
                p(style="margin: 0;") Phone: 01234 567890
            

        //- Date
        p 19 September 2026

        //- HMRC Address (Left Side)
        div(style="margin-bottom: 20px;")
            p(style="margin: 0;") HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC) 
            p(style="margin: 0;") Concept House
            p(style="margin: 0;") 5 Young Street
            p(style="margin: 0;") Sheffield, S1 4LA
            p Phone: 0300 200 3300
      

        //- Salutation
        p Dear Mr Yates,

        //- Subject Line (Optional but recommended for clarity)
        h4 Subject: Taxable Income
        p You may have already heard that as of April this year (05/04/2026), His Majesty's Government implemented changes to the taxation system, and as a result, any benefits over the current personal allowance (currently £12,570) will be subject to taxation at the basic rate of 20%.
        h4 Self Assessment Tax Return
        p You will be required to submit a Self Assessment tax return to report any taxable income above the personal allowance. For the current tax year, you are required to submit your tax return by 31 January 2028. Failure to do so may result in penalties and interest on any unpaid tax.

        h4 Calculating your taxable income
        p For example, if your total income for the year is £20,000, the taxable amount above the personal allowance (£12,570) would be £7,430. This amount would be subject to taxation at the basic rate of 20%, resulting in a tax liability of £7430 / 100 * 20 = £1,486.
        .tax-deductables
            .left
                h4 uk self assessment tax deductibles 
                p In instances where the claimant is receiving Incapacity to Work benefits, or Personal Independence Payment (PIP), certain related expenses may also be deductible. Examples of which may be: 
                ul
                    li Travel expenses related to medical appointments
                    li Costs for specialized equipment or home modifications
                    li Professional advice or support services related to the condition
                    li Gym membership or physical therapy related to the condition
                    li Sports attire 
                    li Herbal remedies related to the condition*
                    
            p.info-box In the UK, certain expenses and allowances can be deducted from your taxable income when completing your Self Assessment tax return. Typically common deductible items include business expenses, charitable donations, and pension contributions. 
            p.starred * It is the duty of HMRC to provide a tax system fair and transparent for all taxpayers, as such illicit drugs will be treated on a case by case basis. An example of such would be any hunger supressing drugs e.g amphetamine, where the annual cost of which would be considered for taxation purposes.
